From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:08:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] expression: parse binary operators in one frame by precedence

The grammar has one production per precedence level and boa had a parser
for each, so every operand, operator or not, went through nine nested
frames (ShortCircuitExpression down to MultiplicativeExpression). On wasm
that was about half of the ~9 KiB of shadow stack one parenthesized
nesting level cost; 0.22's wider parse results made each frame bigger
than 0.20's. reCAPTCHA's recaptcha__en.js nests `this.a=(this.b=(...`
~75 deep inside a function and overran the 1 MiB stack, and the trap
left the wasm module terminated for the rest of the page.

One loop with an explicit stack of pending operators parses the same
language in one frame. `a || b || c` keeps the right-leaning tree boa
always built, so the AST is unchanged for everything boa accepted: 281
npm bundles and 200k generated expressions digest identically, errors
included. `#x in o` may now be followed by another relational operator,
which the spec and V8 allow and the old early return rejected.

Deepest `o.p=(o.p=(...))` boa-bench compiles: 100 -> 174 (0.20: 130).
